    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "properties": {
        "name": "Beach Acess (west)",
        "category": "general",
        "info": "\u003Cp\u003E\nThe Long Island Sound is a 110-mile-long tidal estuary of the Atlantic Ocean. New York and Connecticut are to the North; Long Island is to the South. Saltwater from the ocean mixes with fresh water from Hempstead Harbor and other smaller inland waterways, making it a unique and critical resource for over 120 species of fish, as well as crabs, lobsters, birds, and other marine life. In addition, the Long Island Sound supports a nearly $10 billion dollar economy through coastal recreation, tourism, and commercial fishing.\n\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\nThe largest threats to the Long Island Sound are sediment and nutrient runoff, rising sea levels and temperatures, and plastic pollution. These human effects on the Sound upset the delicate ecosystem balance and destroy the water’s plant and animal diversity. You can help by reducing the use of chemicals in your own lawn care (which ultimately drain into the Sound), planting rain gardens that work to trap runoff before it reaches the Sound, and participating in local beach clean-ups to clear harmful litter from the coast.\n\u003C/p\u003E",
        "url": null,
        "icon": "beach.svg"
      },
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Point",
        "coordinates": [-73.7009689980767, 40.8648685371124]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "properties": {
        "name": "Beach Acess (east)",
        "category": "general",
        "info": "\u003Cp\u003E\nThe Long Island Sound is a 110-mile-long tidal estuary of the Atlantic Ocean. New York and Connecticut are to the North; Long Island is to the South. Saltwater from the ocean mixes with fresh water from Hempstead Harbor and other smaller inland waterways, making it a unique and critical resource for over 120 species of fish, as well as crabs, lobsters, birds, and other marine life. In addition, the Long Island Sound supports a nearly $10 billion dollar economy through coastal recreation, tourism, and commercial fishing.\n\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\nThe largest threats to the Long Island Sound are sediment and nutrient runoff, rising sea levels and temperatures, and plastic pollution. These human effects on the Sound upset the delicate ecosystem balance and destroy the water’s plant and animal diversity. You can help by reducing the use of chemicals in your own lawn care (which ultimately drain into the Sound), planting rain gardens that work to trap runoff before it reaches the Sound, and participating in local beach clean-ups to clear harmful litter from the coast.\n\u003C/p\u003E",
        "url": null,
        "icon": "beach.svg"
      },
